<title>SWEDEN: SAS fleet plans untouched by U.S. merger.</title>
<headline>SAS fleet plans untouched by U.S. merger.</headline>
<dateline>STOCKHOLM 1996-12-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>Scandinavian Airlines (SAS) said on Monday that the announced merger of U.S. plane makers Boeing and McDonnell Douglas would not affect its purchasing plans.</p>
<p>&quot;We will not be affected, we will get the planes we have ordered,&quot; SAS spokesman Knut Lovstuhagen told Reuters.</p>
<p>SAS has orderered six MD90 from McDonnell Douglas, of which two have been delivered, and 41 Boeing 737-600 from Boeing, which will be delivered in the autumn of 1998, Lovstuhagen said.</p>
<p>Traditionally SAS has used McDonnell Douglas as a supplier, but recently it has also turned to Boeing, Lovstuhagen said.</p>
<p>&quot;When we will change our long-distance fleet, we will of course also look at Airbus,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>-- Stockholm newsroom, +46-8-700 1006</p>
